# Personal rules synchronisation

Canonical: `docs/Personal_Rules.md` sections 1 and 20.

- When a rule is added, changed, or removed, update the matching Cursor rules in the same piece of work. Do not leave stale instructions enforcing an old requirement.
- One concern per `.mdc`. Update an existing concern where possible; create a separate rule for a new concern. Remove or shrink rules when their requirements are removed.
- Keep `project-core.mdc` concise and always applied. Use file patterns for file-specific rules and precise task descriptions for workflow rules.
- Keep the actual personal-guide path in references and file patterns consistent: `docs/Personal_Rules.md`.
- When the guide version changes, update the version in `project-core.mdc` and applicable version references.
- Keep existing root `AGENTS.md` and `CLAUDE.md` references consistent when those files are within the authorised scope. Do not create additional tool templates during a Cursor-only task; report follow-up synchronisation needed outside that scope.
- Do not invent technical standards from existing code. Record routine choices in `docs/Assumptions.md`; obtain owner agreement for changes to the standard.
- Increment the major rules version for changes requiring rework of a compliant project; increment the minor version for additions or clarifications. Update the review date after reviewing the complete guide.
- For substantial rule changes, retain the guide's validation requirement: the same fictional request and rules, fresh folders, at least two supported coding agents, equal information, scored outcomes, and recorded failures/decisions. Do not claim this validation has run when it has not.
- A reusable template update does not authorise editing other project repositories. Install and synchronise each project's rules deliberately.
